Same behavior, two syntaxes — this is exactly what async/await desugars into:

```javascript
// Promise chain
function getUserPosts(userId) {
  return fetchUser(userId)
    .then(user => fetchPosts(user.id))
    .then(posts => posts.filter(p => p.published));
}

// async/await — same behavior, linear-looking code
async function getUserPosts(userId) {
  const user = await fetchUser(userId);
  const posts = await fetchPosts(user.id);
  return posts.filter(p => p.published);
}
```

Both versions yield control back to the event loop at each asynchronous step, and neither blocks the thread while `fetchUser`/`fetchPosts` are pending. The async/await version is easier to read — especially once error handling and branching enter the picture, where promise chains get visually nested and hard to follow, while `try`/`catch` around `await` reads like ordinary synchronous error handling.

A common pattern that trips people up: awaiting several independent operations *sequentially* when they could run concurrently.

```javascript
// Sequential: waits for each in turn — 300ms total if each takes 100ms
const a = await fetchA();
const b = await fetchB();
const c = await fetchC();

// Concurrent: all three start immediately, wait for all — ~100ms total
const [a, b, c] = await Promise.all([fetchA(), fetchB(), fetchC()]);
```

The first version accidentally serializes independent work because each `await` blocks *that task* from proceeding until its own promise resolves, even though nothing stops `fetchB` from starting before `fetchA` finishes. `Promise.all` (or Python's `asyncio.gather`) starts every operation immediately and awaits all of them together — the fix is almost always this pattern, and its absence is a common, easy-to-miss source of unnecessary latency.
